  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nLower clouds linger along the Int'l border today associated with \nthe low pressure over W TX. Very little chance of rain for \nHudspeth County this afternoon as drier air filters in from the \nnorth and erodes much of the cloud cover. The low slides to the \neast by Tue and opens up into a shortwave, taking most of the \nmoisture with it. Areas of low clouds/fog may redevelop Tue AM \nunder the clearing skies and calm winds. Otherwise, mainly clear \nwith light winds for Tue as upper ridging moves in from the west.\n\nThe ridge becomes more amplified Wed/Thu and shifts overhead, \nkeeping us mainly clear with generally light winds. A weak \nbackdoor front arrives on Wed, bringing modestly breezy N-NE winds\nthat day. \n\nA shortwave trough pushes in from the Pacific by Sat associated \nwith a negatively tilted longwave. Increasing clouds are forecast \nfor Fri ahead of the disturbance, followed by a low chance of rain\nFri night-Sat. Global models and ensembles have sped up the \nsystem, so Sun now looks dry. Mostly light rain showers are \nforecast with minimal threat of flash flooding. A few \nthunderstorms can't be ruled out as the LREF shows modest \ninstability Fri evening along with PVA. Most likely rain amounts \nare <0.1\" as southern areas are favored to see precip. Snow levels\nwill be high (9-10kft) during the event, so accumulations are not\nexpected, although a rain/snow mix can be seen on the highest \nelevations through Sat. The shortwave departs to the east on Sun, \nleaving us with another ridge of high pressure to end the period. \nTemperatures hold near normal, cooling slightly for the weekend.",
